# Native macOS fullscreen Spaces

Work on macos-fullscreen; Client only. Preserve the accepted Host and Linux
presentation, protocol, Retina Match Client and exact-format decode paths.

## Finding and approach

Current decision (Client 1.0.119): the operator tested all standalone probe
modes. They had no side borders but retained the same native top notch strip.
The operator accepts that system-owned strip, including its protection for
remote menus. Stop pursuing full-panel overrides. Keep native Spaces/swipes;
fullscreen Match Client now subtracts NSScreen.safeAreaInsets.top from logical
and backing heights at the existing Retina scale. Zero inset means the entire
display; do not subtract Dock/menu visibleFrame or hardcode a laptop size.
Authentication and session/reconnect validation share this geometry. Preserve
the full display bounds separately for screen identity/window placement.
Manual resolutions and windowed startup retain existing aspect/sizing policy;
toggling window mode does not renegotiate the active Host resolution.
Remove the ineffective SDL patch/helper/hint and invalidate dependency cache.
No Host/Linux behavior change. Candidate still needs notched and non-notched
live checks; compiled geometry tests and source guards are not visual proof.

The following investigation is retained as evidence, not an active build policy.

Issue15 follow-up: remove the old horizontal toolbar notch avoidance. Native
fullscreen already places the content below the camera area, so the toolbar
uses ordinary centered initial placement and preserves dragged positions on
layout refresh. Keep the fullscreen top inset, Retina geometry, reveal timing,
pinning and input routing unchanged. Verify centered reveal, dragging and
fullscreen/windowed transitions on a notched laptop before visual acceptance.
The older auxiliary-area placement described below is superseded.

Candidate 1.0.119 passed signed hosted run 35131997746 from root
9167fd8412172fee9d47be9eb2bc67cc155d7750 / Client
6f0c25269c5a8bf1051814317440f2cd57fda005. Cold bootstrap, compiled geometry,
19 topology / 21 toolbar / seven desktop-stage tests, native compilation and
distribution gates passed. HANDOFF records the verified DMG checksum/location.
The operator accepted 1.0.119 and authorized merging and a full mainline rebuild
as 1.0.120. No Host behavior change. Acceptance is operator-reported; do not
infer separate coverage of every monitor/input combination.

Client 1.0.114 disabled SDL's native fullscreen Spaces while also opting out
of display safe-area compatibility. This fixed full-panel coverage but removed
normal fullscreen Space participation. SDL 3.4.2's Cocoa delegate accepts
AppKit's proposed fullscreen content size without an application override.

Restore Spaces and request the actual NSScreen frame size through AppKit's
documented window:willUseFullScreenContentSize: delegate callback. Implement
that narrow opt-in inside the pinned SDL Cocoa delegate, not a replacement
delegate, runtime swizzle, gesture interceptor or manual window-movement loop.
Do not switch display modes, stretch/crop video or hardcode a notch dimension.
Keep the safe-area compatibility opt-out and existing dynamic toolbar placement.

The SDL patch is a required build input: apply-or-prove-applied at bootstrap,
independently verify before every build, and include it in dependency cache
fingerprints. An unpatched prepared tree must fail. Other SDL users retain
upstream behavior unless the PLANK-specific hint is enabled.

## Validation

Check patch application/reverse verification and source wiring, native build,
shared toolbar/topology/reconnect tests and signed/notarized package gates.
Log the proposed/fullscreen screen size and settled native content/drawable
geometry to distinguish an AppKit restriction from renderer letterboxing.
The AppKit sizing override is a candidate, not proof of notch behavior: the
hosted builder has no operator laptop/notch/trackpad acceptance coverage.

Operator checks before merge: swipe between fullscreen apps and back; verify
full-panel Retina Match Client, sharp text, accurate mouse/pen positions,
toolbar reveal/drag, held-key release on focus loss, window/fullscreen toggle,
minimize/restore and disconnect. Also check a non-notched display/manual mode.
No new Host package is required. Do not merge without operator acceptance.

## Candidate

Signed Client 1.0.117-macos-fullscreen passed hosted run 35124970815 from root
aea1ab33e7c03ebf8c9ee67e36239beb81261217 / Client
509f2fc73718b3dffdb9fd9ad9c03065bc14b6c2. Patch/prepared-library verification,
six focused guards, shared Qt suites, package closure/version, signing,
notarization and Gatekeeper pass. The exact hash-verified DMG is in the
candidate catalog; HANDOFF records its checksum. Operator installed it and
confirmed swipe switching works, but the top/side borders returned. No merge.
Keep the accepted Host unchanged.

## Live result and next decision

The sizing callback executes: proposed 1710x1073, requested 1710x1107 points.
After the native transition, frame and content are still 1710x1073 with a
3420x2146 drawable. The stream is 3420x2214. Aspect fitting that stream to the
shorter drawable explains the side borders; the missing top area is outside
the native window. These are observed dimensions, not constants to encode.

The callback is insufficient. Do not add blind resize loops, private AppKit
hooks, cropping or distortion. Native fullscreen with Match Client based on
the usable content area is the simpler alternative: it would remove the side
borders while preserving Retina scale and gestures, but retain the system
notch strip. That differs from full-panel coverage and needs operator agreement.
Otherwise investigate and prove a full-panel native-Space architecture before
another product candidate. Custom animation alone is not established as a fix.

## Follow-up investigation

The operator requested continued investigation of full-panel coverage WITH
native Spaces, not acceptance of the reduced viewport. No production code was
changed during this investigation.

Source comparisons:

- Mozilla bug 1737953 comment 18 describes the same system-owned top strip
  in native fullscreen, including Safari. This supports the log diagnosis,
  not a universal claim that every alternative architecture is impossible.
- Helium's notch-fullscreen demo explicitly bypasses native fullscreen in its
  controller. It is not proof of a full-panel native Space.
- Ghostty separates native and non-native fullscreen implementations; its
  borderless mode fills the screen. It is not the missing combined solution.
- mpv's macOS Window subclass uses native toggleFullScreen, custom fullscreen
  animation to the screen frame, AND a constrainFrameRect override during
  fullscreen/transition. SDL3.4.2 lacks that override. Apple documents overriding
  constrainFrameRect to change frame constraints. This is a concrete single-
  window hypothesis, NOT confirmed notch behavior on macOS 27. IINA's custom
  animation alone must not be taken as evidence; it also has an open notch issue.

Next diagnostic should be a standalone AppKit window probe comparing stock
native fullscreen against custom animation, frame-constraint override, and
their combination. Log proposed/constrained/settled frame and backing content
dimensions, including after swiping away/back and fullscreen exit/re-entry.
Use current screen geometry; no fixed notch dimensions, private selectors,
method swizzling, resizing timers, display mode changes or global settings.
Success requires visible full-panel content AND native Space switching; merely
returning a larger rectangle or compiling successfully is not success.

If that fails, a separate native fullscreen owner plus attached borderless
presentation window is a possible second probe: Apple's fullScreenAuxiliary
and addChildWindow APIs provide the attachment primitives. Their documentation
does NOT establish notch coverage or correct swipe animation. That approach
adds focus/input/cleanup complexity and must not enter PLANK without a proof.
No notched-Mac execution was available in this research turn; a hosted compile
alone cannot close the live geometry/gesture gate.

## Standalone probe delivery

`probes/macos/fullscreen-window.m` now implements five single-window modes.
All enter native Spaces. It has private geometry-only logs, visual edge markers,
an explicit mode selector and normal Quit/Escape controls, no product/network
dependency and no system setting writes. Full test instructions are alongside
the source in `fullscreen-window.md`.

The 1.0.118-macos-fullscreen diagnostic passed signed hosted run 35129303592,
root 21c295446892c6d0a6bb57c326040e94763871c1. Strict native compilation and
distribution gates passed; the downloaded hash matches the runner. HANDOFF
records the separate `artifacts/diagnostics/` path/checksum. No new Client or
Host package was produced. Live notched-Mac coverage and swipe tests remain
pending; do not integrate an experimental policy based on this build alone.

